A||||4||Ronen||On engine compartment cooling ideas||||3||
Z||000000||Ronen||05-21-2001||05:32 PM||||Does anyone have an opinion on this? When I take my trip from Boise, ID to Denver, CO in mid-July I will take off the back engine cover to allow the engine to run colder.||||reg||3
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||05-21-2001||05:56 PM||||Hey Ronen!<BR>If you are traveling at normal hiway speeds, it shouldn't be a problem...<BR>If it is stop and go all the way, then I would worry...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||BV MotorSports||05-21-2001||06:09 PM||||Do you have a problem with your fiero running hot? A car should run at its coolest at a sustained hwy speed since the engine load is very minimal at cruise.<BR>Minor tricks to improve engine cooling are:<BR>Remove the louvered metal below the deck lid louvers.<BR>Add a 160 or 180 thermostat<BR>Add a cool fan switch.<P>I use the 160 t-stat and low temp fan switch and my car NEVER gets over 160 deg.<BR>Problem is that it stays TOO cold in the winter (never moves past 100!!!)<BR>So keep your old t-stat and fan switch to swap over to in the cooler months or buy the 180 and the higher temp cool fan switch (still cooler than stock by 20 deg).<P>Other than that have your cooling system checked and serviced by a professional.<P>Steven||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||05-21-2001||06:50 PM||||If you have any questions about the temp in the engine compartment, do what I did: I took an indoor/outdoor thermometer I had with a remote sensor, and put the sensor in the engine compartment. I have NO vent on mine (I prefer a cleaner look) and even with no vents, according to the thermometer, it only gets as hot as the engine. And when cruising, it barely ever goes over 135 degrees.||||reg||4
Z||000004||cscougar||05-21-2001||07:43 PM||||I live in Central Texas and it really gets hot here.  I went with the 180 degree thermostat and the 180 degree fan switch from Rodney Dickman.  My 85 GT runs cool as can be.   No other adjustments necessary.<BR>||||reg||1
